.\" Mess man page
.TH MESS 1 "__DATE__" "mess __VERSION__" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mess \- wrap a pager and navigate OSC8 links
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mess
.RI [ pager-args ]
.br
.B mess
.B -m
.RI [ pager-args ]
.br
.B mess
.B --open
.RI < target >
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mess
launches a pager (by default
.BR less (1))
inside a pseudo terminal, intercepts its output, and enables keyboard
navigation for OSC8 hyperlinks on POSIX systems such as macOS, the BSDs, and
Linux. It relies on common utilities including
.BR less (1),
.BR lynx (1),
and
.BR lowdown (1)
as helpers. All command line arguments other than
.B --open
and
.B --help
are forwarded to the pager unchanged.
.PP
When the pager emits OSC8 hyperlinks,
.B mess
records them and shows the currently selected link on the last terminal
line. Press the Tab key to cycle through the list of links, press Enter to
open the highlighted link, or press Escape to clear the selection and resume
normal pager input. Any other key is passed straight to the pager.
.PP
If the first non option argument names a Markdown file,
.B mess
executes
.BR lowdown (1)
to render the document to the terminal and pipes the generated stream into
the pager so that hyperlinks stay active. Supplying a
.I file://
URL performs the same conversion once the scheme and optional fragment are
stripped.
.PP
When the input stream is a manual page,
.B mess
looks for tokens such as
.I printf(3)
or
.I socket(2)
and exposes them as navigation targets. Cycling through links now highlights
those references directly in the pager, and activating them opens the matching
.I man://
URI.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ mess README.md            # page a regular file with link navigation
$ mess --open https://bsd.lv/projects # open a URL without starting the pager
$ mess --open man://ls.1    # resolve and view ls(1) through the dispatcher
$ MANPAGER='mess -m' man 7 signal    # keep manual cross references interactive
.fi
.PP
Set
.B PAGER=mess
system wide when you want the wrapper to stand in for
.BR less (1);
export
.B MESSPAGER="less -R"
to point downstream at your preferred pager so recursive invocations keep
working.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.B mess
targets POSIX systems such as macOS 12 or newer, the BSD family, and modern
Linux distributions with glibc 2.31 or musl 1.2. Building requires a C11
compiler (for example Clang 14+ or GCC 11+) and
.BR make (1)
so the bundled
.B Makefile
can enforce the default
.B -Wall -Wextra -Werror
flags. Runtime behavior depends on standard utilities:
.BR less (1)
or another pager,
.BR mdcat (1)
or
.BR lowdown (1)
to render Markdown,
.BR lynx (1)
for inline browsing, and a graphical opener such as
.BR open (1)
or
.BR xdg-open (1).
The dispatcher honors the
.B PAGER
,
.B MESSPAGER
,
.B BROWSER
, and
.B MESS_MDRENDER
environment variables when selecting these helpers, and the integration test
suite additionally uses
.BR tikl (1)
to drive dispatcher fixtures.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --open " " target
Open
.I target
without launching the interactive pager. Markdown inputs are rendered through
.BR lowdown (1),
.I file://
URLs are normalized and reopened in
.BR mess ,
strings beginning with
.I man://
are resolved via
.BR man (1)
so
.I man://mess.1
looks up
.BR mess (1),
and other targets are dispatched to a web browser.
.TP
.BR --man ", " -m
Force the navigation layer to treat piped input as a manual page even when no
file extension or `man://` scheme is available. Useful when wiring `mess` into
.B MANPAGER
so `man` output keeps the richer link navigation.
.TP
.BR --help ", " -h
Print a short usage summary.
.TP
.BR --version ", " -V
Display the built-in version string and exit.
.SH NAVIGATION
When OSC8 hyperlinks are present the last terminal line shows the currently
selected entry prefixed with
.BR => .
Use the following keys while the pager is running:
.TP
.B Tab / j
Cycle through the discovered links in display order.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the highlighted link using the configured launcher (see
.B BROWSER
under
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
.TP
.B Shift-Tab / k
Cycle backwards through the discovered links.
.TP
.B v
Open the document referenced by the
.B MESSFILE
environment variable in the editor configured via
.B VISUAL
or, if unset,
.BR EDITOR .
Once the editor exits
.B mess
requests a redraw from the pager to refresh the screen. When
.B mess
is launched with a positional file/URI it sets
.B MESSFILE
before spawning the pager; wrapper mode (`... | mess`) can export it manually
so the shortcut knows which path to edit.
.TP
.B c
Toggle inline link coloring, assigning a distinct ANSI color to each stored
link so OSC8 targets and manual cross references stand out while browsing.
.TP
.B x
Toggle the one second capture freeze. When enabled (the default) `mess` asks the
pager for a refresh the first time you hit Tab, captures the resulting redraw
for one second, and then freezes the terminal so the highlighted links stay
stable; a `messF` indicator appears on the top right while navigation is active,
and a trailing `!` warns when more pager output arrived but was suppressed by
the freeze.
.TP
.B D
Toggle a debugging overlay that lists every stored link and its target, useful
when validating OSC8 output or manual cross references.
.TP
.B Escape
Clear the selection and resume normal pager input.
.TP
.B Ctrl-L
Exit navigation mode without launching the current link, then request a pager
refresh.
.PP
Any other key is forwarded to the pager unchanged. The selection persists until
cleared explicitly or a new buffer is viewed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PAGER
Overrides the pager command. When the first word is
.BR less ,
.B mess
automatically appends
.B -r
unless a raw output flag is already present.
.B MESSPAGER
is consulted before
.B PAGER
so you can reserve a separate command for the wrapper; when you export
.B PAGER=mess
system-wide, set
.B MESSPAGER
to your real pager (for example `less -R`) so the dispatcher knows what to run
downstream.
.TP
.B BROWSER
Command used when opening links or non Markdown targets. The value is
expanded using
.BR wordexp (3).
Fallbacks are
.BR open (1)
on macOS and
.BR xdg-open (1)
elsewhere.
.TP
.B MANPAGER
When dispatching manual pages,
.B mess
sets
.B MANPAGER
to `mess -m` so cross references remain navigable. Override it if you prefer a
different pager wrapper in that scenario.
.TP
.BR VISUAL ", " EDITOR
Command invoked when pressing
.B v
during navigation. The value is expanded via
.BR wordexp (3)
before appending the current document path. The default fallback is the
traditional
.BR vi (1)
when neither variable is set.
.TP
.B MESSFILE
Absolute or relative path to the document currently shown in the pager. The
dispatcher mode (`mess <file|uri>`) sets this automatically so the
.B v
shortcut knows what to edit. When feeding the pager via stdin you can export
.B MESSFILE
ahead of time to give navigation mode the same context.
.TP
.B MESS_MDRENDER
Markdown renderer to apply before piping the output into the pager when opening
`.md` files directly. The default command is
.BR "lowdown -tterm --term-no-links" .
Point this variable at another converter (for example `pandoc -t plain`) to
customize the preview.
.TP
.B MESS_DEBUG_STATUS
Optional path that enables logging of every status-line update emitted by
navigation mode. Helpful when debugging terminal control sequences; unset by
default so no extra files are created.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B mess
exits 0 when the pager and any helper processes finish successfully.
Failures to spawn helpers, pager errors, or signal induced aborts result in a
non zero status.
.PP
Common exit scenarios include quitting the pager normally, consuming all input
from a pipe, receiving consecutive interrupts (the second interrupt forces an
abort), or encountering system I/O errors while reading and writing the PTY.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR less (1),
.BR lowdown (1),
.BR lynx (1),
.BR xdg-open (1)
